- Avoid routine NSAIDs for back pain without discussing bleeding risk on apixaban; ask about safer pain options.
- If you have anemia, discuss whether it’s stable and whether anticoagulation is still appropriate or needs monitoring more frequently.

When to consider referral to cardiology (reasonable triggers)
- New or recurrent atrial fibrillation, symptomatic arrhythmia, or difficult rate control.
- Unexplained syncope, recurrent palpitations, or concerning ECG changes.
- Resistant hypertension (BP above goal despite 3 meds of different classes including a diuretic).
- Structural heart disease on echo, reduced ejection fraction, or significant valvular disease.
- Complex anticoagulation questions where cardiology’s input will influence long-term therapy (e.g., need for left atrial appendage evaluation, recurrent thromboembolism while anticoagulated).
- Otherwise, at age 41 without the above findings, routine cardiology referral is not automatically required — it depends on the above factors and your overall ASCVD risk.

Quick visit checklist (what to bring)
- Updated med list (drug, dose, frequency) and reasons for each.
- Recent home BP log (2 weeks if available) and any symptomatic episodes.
- List of current symptoms and duration.
- Copies or dates of recent labs (creatinine/eGFR, CBC, lipids, HbA1c).
- Any prior cardiac tests (ECG, echo) or DEXA scan results.

