  - “For osteopenia, should I get a DEXA scan (if not recent) and any bone-protective therapy or fall-risk counseling?”

Suggested tests you might ask to have done before/at the visit
- Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, eGFR, electrolytes)
- CBC (to evaluate anemia)
- Lipid panel
- HbA1c or fasting glucose
- TSH (if clinically relevant)
- ECG (resting)
- Ambulatory 24-hour BP monitor or instructions for home BP log
- Iron studies / B12 / folate (if anemia not recently evaluated)
- DEXA scan (if not done in last 1–2 years, depending on prior DEXA and risk)
- Urinalysis (proteinuria screening)
- If symptoms or abnormal ECG: echocardiogram or stress testing as indicated

When cardiology referral is commonly recommended (talk with your PCP about these triggers)
- Resistant hypertension: BP not controlled despite 3 antihypertensives of different classes (one should be a diuretic), or controlled only with ≥4 medications
- Recurrent syncope, unexplained palpitations, documented arrhythmia (e.g., AF), or new-onset atrial fibrillation
- Evidence of structural heart disease (abnormal ECG/echo, heart failure, valvular disease)
- Ischemic symptoms (chest pain) or positive cardiac stress testing
- Unclear cause of very high BP or suspected secondary hypertension

Practical appointment checklist — things to bring or prepare
- Current medication list (names, doses, and when you take them) and pill bottles if possible
- BP log or recent home readings (times and values)
- Any recent lab results or imaging (bring copies or have portal access ready)
- List of symptoms and when they occur (dizzy, palpitations, chest pain, shortness of breath)
- Family history of heart disease, stroke, or early cardiovascular events
- Ask whether you should stop or adjust any meds before tests (rare, but confirm if labs need fasting)

