  - Suspected secondary hypertension (young age with severe HTN, abrupt onset, refractory).
  - Documented arrhythmia (e.g., AF) or ongoing need to manage anticoagulation in context of structural heart disease.
  - New or unexplained syncope, recurrent palpitations, exertional chest pain or dyspnea, or heart failure symptoms.
  - Abnormal ECG, evidence of cardiac ischemia, or reduced ejection fraction on prior testing.
- If you don’t meet these, your PCP can usually manage BP and decide on cardiology referral later if concerns arise.

Tests and measurements to request at the visit
- BP measured properly (sitting, multiple readings; orthostatic vitals if symptomatic)
- Serum BMP (electrolytes, creatinine) and eGFR
- CBC (evaluate anemia)
- Lipid panel, fasting glucose or A1c
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney damage assessment)
- ECG (baseline rhythm if not done recently, especially given apixaban)
- Consider echocardiogram if symptoms, abnormal exam/ECG, or to evaluate structure/function
- DEXA scan follow-up for osteopenia if due; calcium/vit D levels as appropriate
- Iron studies, B12, folate if anemia cause is unclear

Lifestyle and non-pharmacologic items to discuss
- Salt reduction (DASH-style diet), weight loss if applicable, regular aerobic exercise (aim ~150 min/wk), limit alcohol, stop smoking.
- Sleep apnea screening if snoring/excessive daytime sleepiness (OSA increases BP).
- Fall-prevention and bone-health strategies given osteopenia.

Red flags that should prompt urgent cardiology/ED evaluation
- Chest pain, new severe shortness of breath, fainting/syncope, signs of stroke (sudden weakness, slurred speech), or major bleeding.

Suggested wording if you want to ask Dr. Wilson for a cardiology referral
- “Given my history (age 41, osteopenia, anemia, on apixaban and metoprolol) and my recent BP readings, would you consider a cardiology evaluation at Colorado Cardiology to assess cardiovascular risk and optimize BP/anticoagulation management? If you think it’s appropriate, could you refer me?”

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short message/email you can send to Dr. Wilson summarizing these questions and requesting a referral if indicated?
- Make a one-page checklist you can bring to the appointment with the tests and questions listed?

If you can tell me the reason for apixaban and clarify the creatinine value, I can make the guidance more specific.

