This is a great time to prepare. I can’t give medical advice, but I can help you organize the right questions, tests, and red flags to discuss with Dr. James Wilson so you can decide together whether cardiology referral is needed. A few quick clarifying questions first, then I’ll give suggested questions to ask, tests to request, what would prompt a cardiology referral, and practical next steps.

Quick clarifying questions
- Do you know why you’re on apixaban (e.g., atrial fibrillation, prior clot)?  
- When you wrote “last creatinine was 72%,” do you mean eGFR 72 mL/min/1.73m2, serum creatinine 72 µmol/L, or something else? (This matters for med choices and apixaban dosing.)  
- Do you have recent home BP readings (numbers and times) or clinic readings?  
- Any symptoms lately: chest pain, unexplained shortness of breath, dizziness, syncope, palpitations, worsening back pain with exertion, or falls?  
- Any known coronary artery disease, heart failure, or prior ECG/echo abnormalities?

Suggested questions to ask Dr. Wilson about your hypertension
- What blood pressure target do you recommend for me, given my age (41) and other conditions (osteopenia, anemia, on apixaban and metoprolol)?  
- Are my current home/clinic BP readings at goal? If not, what changes do you recommend (medication adjustment, dosing changes, or lifestyle)?  
- Is metoprolol 25 mg still the best choice for my BP and heart-rate goals? Are there benefits or downsides right now given my anemia and back pain?  
- Does my anticoagulation (apixaban) affect BP targets or the urgency of tighter BP control? (High uncontrolled BP increases bleeding risk on anticoagulants.)  
- Should we check for orthostatic hypotension before changing meds (given possible dizziness or falls)?  
- Do you want a 24-hour ambulatory BP monitor or home BP log to confirm uncontrolled hypertension or white-coat effect?  
- What labs or tests should we run before changing antihypertensives (kidney function, electrolytes, etc.)?  
- Given my other conditions, would a thiazide-type diuretic help (may reduce calcium loss), or are there reasons to avoid it?  
- At what point would you consider referral to Colorado Cardiology?

Recommended tests and data to bring to the visit
- Recent BP log: home readings (time of day, position—sitting/standing) for 1–2 weeks if possible.  
- Recent labs: BMP/CMP (creatinine, eGFR, electrolytes), CBC (for anemia), lipid panel, A1c (if applicable), TSH. Also urine albumin/creatinine ratio if not recently done.  
- ECG (if not recent) — useful baseline for rate/rhythm and to check for atrial fibrillation if that’s the reason for apixaban.  
- Past cardiac tests if any (previous ECGs, echocardiogram, stress test, cardiology notes).  
- Medication list including doses, and OTC/supplements.

Why you might need cardiology referral
Consider referral if any of these apply:
- Resistant hypertension (BP above target on three antihypertensives of different classes, one being a diuretic)  
- Suspected secondary hypertension (young age with very high BP, sudden onset, or suggestive labs)  
- Documented or suspected arrhythmia (e.g., AF) or symptoms like frequent palpitations/syncope  
- Signs of structural heart disease, heart failure symptoms (exertional dyspnea, edema), or ischemic symptoms  
- Complex anticoagulation issues or need for procedures that require cardiology input  
- Unexplained tachycardia/bradycardia or unclear cause of anemia affecting cardiac status  
- If your PCP thinks advanced testing (echocardiogram, ambulatory rhythm monitor, invasive studies) is needed

Medication and condition interactions to mention
- Apixaban: ensure renal function (eGFR) is up to date for safe dosing; uncontrolled severe hypertension increases bleeding risk on anticoagulants.  
- Metoprolol: can reduce heart rate and BP — discuss whether dose adjustments are needed, especially if you feel lightheaded or have exercise intolerance.  
- Osteopenia: thiazide diuretics can reduce urinary calcium loss (sometimes beneficial), while loop diuretics can increase calcium loss — discuss bone health when changing meds.  
- Anemia: can cause tachycardia, fatigue, or exertional symptoms that mimic cardiac issues — check the cause and severity.

What to ask specifically about a cardiology referral to Colorado Cardiology
- Do you recommend cardiology evaluation for my risk profile, or can you manage my hypertension?  
- If you refer me, what specifically should cardiology evaluate (resistant HTN, arrhythmia, echo, coronary risk)?  
- Will I need any tests done first (ECG, labs, ambulatory BP monitor) before the referral?

Red flags — contact PCP or urgent care now if you have:
- New or worsening chest pain, new shortness of breath at rest or with minimal exertion, fainting/syncope, sudden severe headache, sudden weakness or numbness — especially important on apixaban (bleeding/stroke risk).

Practical next steps I can help with
- Draft a concise message or visit note for Dr. Wilson summarizing your conditions, meds (apixaban, metoprolol), recent creatinine value (please clarify), home BP readings, and asking whether he recommends cardiology referral.  
- Prepare a printable checklist of tests/answers to bring to your appointment.  
- If you provide your recent BP readings and lab values (especially creatinine/eGFR, lipids, CBC), I can help estimate ASCVD risk and suggest questions tailored to your numbers.

Would you like me to draft a short message/email to Dr. Wilson asking whether he recommends cardiology referral and listing the tests you want checked? If so, please confirm the creatinine/eGFR value and the indication for apixaban (if you know it).

