- Basic metabolic panel (BMP): creatinine, electrolytes, and eGFR — confirm current renal function (important for apixaban dosing and some BP meds)
- CBC (to evaluate anemia)
- Lipid panel
- Hemoglobin A1c or fasting glucose
- TSH (to check for thyroid-related BP changes)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (to check for kidney involvement)
- Resting ECG (if not done recently), and consider ambulatory ECG or Holter if you have palpitations or irregular beats
- 24-hour ambulatory BP monitoring or home BP logs if readings are inconsistent or suspect masked/resistant hypertension
- Orthostatic vital signs in clinic (especially given metoprolol and fall risk)
- DEXA bone density if follow-up is needed for osteopenia (if not recent)

How apixaban, osteopenia, anemia, and metoprolol affect BP decisions
- Anticoagulation (apixaban) increases bleeding risk, so very high uncontrolled BP increases risk of intracranial hemorrhage — controlling BP is important. Also discuss fall risk given osteopenia; if you’re at high fall risk, that factors into anticoagulation risk–benefit discussions.
- Apixaban dosing and safety depend on renal function — confirm eGFR/creatinine to ensure current dose is appropriate.
- Metoprolol can lower heart rate and blood pressure; check resting HR and symptoms of bradycardia or orthostasis.
- Anemia can cause symptoms (fatigue, palpitations, dyspnea) that overlap with cardiac issues — good to evaluate and treat.

