- Hypertension that is resistant (uncontrolled despite 3 medications including a diuretic) or requiring complex regimen changes
- Suspected secondary hypertension (young onset, abrupt worsening, difficult to control)
- Atrial fibrillation, frequent arrhythmias, syncope, or structural heart disease on exam/ECG/echo
- Heart failure symptoms, unexplained chest pain, or ischemic heart disease
- If you want a specialist opinion about anticoagulation vs fall risk or complex medication interactions
- Age 41 alone is not an indication — it’s the combination of resistance, rhythm problems, cardiac structural issues, or symptoms that typically prompt referral

