- Ambulatory BP monitoring if readings vary or to confirm hypertension pattern
- DEXA follow-up and vitamin D level for osteopenia
- Medication reconciliation (exact doses and schedule) and review of bleeding risk for apixaban

6) Short visit checklist you can bring
- Current med list with doses/times (apixaban, metoprolol, supplements)
- Recent BP readings (home) and when taken
- Any recent labs or reports (print or have portal access)
- List of symptoms (dizziness, palpitations, bleeding, syncope)
- Questions above written out (pick 3–5 priority items)
- Ask explicitly for next steps (labs, ECG, referrals) and timeframe for follow-up

7) Suggested short scripts you can use
- To ask about BP goals: “Given my age and conditions, what BP target should we aim for, and are my current home readings OK?”
- To raise referral possibility: “Do you think I need cardiology evaluation (Colorado Cardiology) for my blood pressure/arrhythmia risk, or can we manage here? If referral is needed, could you send one stating the specific reason?”
- To address anemia while on apixaban: “I’m on apixaban and have anemia—can we check CBC and iron studies to rule out bleeding? Should apixaban be re-evaluated?”

8) Safety note
If you experience chest pain, fainting, severe shortness of breath, sudden worsening neurological symptoms, or signs of major bleeding (large bruises, coughing/vomiting blood, black stools), seek emergency care immediately.

