Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
This is a great plan — I can help you prepare focused questions and a checklist to make your annual physical as productive as possible. Before I give a tailored plan, two quick clarifying questions:
- Can you confirm the exact medications & doses (you wrote apixaban 5 mg twice daily — is that correct — and metoprolol 25 mg once daily or twice daily?), and the reason you take apixaban (e.g., atrial fibrillation, DVT/PE)?
- When you say “last creatinine was 72%,” do you mean eGFR 72 mL/min/1.73 m2, serum creatinine 0.72 mg/dL, or something else? If you don’t have the exact number handy, that’s OK — I’ll give general guidance but we’ll want the exact value for dosing questions.

Below are: a one-page checklist to bring to the visit, suggested tests to request, a set of direct questions to ask Dr. James Wilson about hypertension and your cardiovascular risk, and guidance about when a referral to cardiology (e.g., Colorado Cardiology) makes sense.

Checklist to bring to the visit
- Updated medication list with doses, timing, and reason for each (include supplements)
- Recent home BP logs (ideally morning & evening readings for 1–2 weeks)
- Any recent lab results you have (BMP/creatinine, CBC, lipid panel, A1c, recent DEXA)
- Notes on symptoms: chest pain, palpitations, lightheadedness, syncope, worsening shortness of breath, falls, increasing back pain
- Family history of heart disease, stroke, or early sudden death
- List of questions (below) and permission to send records/referral if needed

Tests to consider requesting at the annual physical
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) — creatinine, electrolytes (important for apixaban safety and med dosing)
- CBC (to investigate anemia)
- Iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC) if anemia present or suspected iron deficiency
- Lipid panel
- HbA1c (if diabetes risk or last >1 year)
- TSH (if unexplained anemia or fatigue)
- ECG (12-lead) — especially if apixaban is for AF or if you have palpitations/syncope
- Urinalysis (if indicated)
- DEXA bone density (if not done in last 2 years, because of osteopenia)
- Vitamin D and calcium if bone health hasn’t been checked recently
- Consider ambulatory BP monitoring (24-hour) if readings are inconsistent or to assess white-coat vs sustained HTN

Specific questions to ask Dr. Wilson about hypertension management
- What is my current BP goal given my age, AF/anticoagulation, osteopenia, and other conditions? (General guideline: many adults with cardiovascular risk are targeted to <130/80, but individualization matters.)
- Based on my home BP readings, do you think my current therapy is adequate, or should we adjust medications?
- Are there any interactions or safety concerns between my blood pressure meds and apixaban (or other meds/supplements) that I should watch for?
- Should we check orthostatic BPs given my medications and fall/bleeding risk? (Show any symptoms of lightheadedness or falls.)
- If we need to intensify BP control, would you prefer to change dose of metoprolol, add a different class (ACE inhibitor/ARB, thiazide-like diuretic, calcium channel blocker), or refer to cardiology?
- Do you recommend ambulatory (24-hour) BP monitoring to confirm control?
- How often should I monitor kidney function and electrolytes while on these medicines? (For apixaban dosing and some BP meds you’ll want periodic creatinine checks.)
- Given my age and risk factors, should I have an ECG now or any baseline cardiac imaging (echo) before considering further changes?

Questions about anticoagulation, kidney function, and safety
- My apixaban dose — is it the correct dose for my kidney function/weight/age? (Share the exact creatinine/eGFR and weight.)
- Do I need any special precautions for procedures, dental work, or falls given my osteopenia?
- Do we need to review bleeding risk factors and plan (what to do if I bruise or have trauma)?
- If you have atrial fibrillation or other arrhythmias: is cardiology follow-up recommended for rhythm management or stroke risk review?

When to consider referral to cardiology (Colorado Cardiology)
Consider asking for or accepting a cardiology referral if any of the following apply:
- New or recurrent atrial fibrillation, symptomatic arrhythmias, or palpitations requiring rhythm management
- Chest pain suggesting ischemia or new exertional symptoms
- Poorly controlled hypertension despite 2–3 appropriate agents including a diuretic (so-called resistant hypertension)
- Suspected secondary hypertension (young onset, abrupt resistance to therapy, very high BP)
- Heart failure symptoms (shortness of breath, leg swelling, reduced exercise tolerance)
- Unexplained syncope, recurrent presyncope, or concerning ECG abnormalities
- Complex anticoagulation decisions (e.g., recurrent thromboembolism while on DOAC, need for left atrial appendage evaluation)
- Prior structural heart disease, cardiomyopathy, or prior abnormal echocardiogram

What cardiology will commonly evaluate/do
- 12-lead ECG and possibly ambulatory monitoring (Holter or event monitor)
- Echocardiogram (to look at structure/function)
- Stress testing if ischemia suspected
- Detailed medication optimization for BP and rhythm control, and management of anticoagulation in complex cases
- Recommendations on targets and follow-up intervals

Other issues to review at the visit
- Investigate anemia: ask for CBC + iron studies; if iron deficiency suspected, further GI evaluation may be needed
- Bone health: DEXA frequency and whether to start/adjust osteoporosis meds, calcium/vitamin D
- Back pain: assess mobility, fall risk, impact on daily activities — coordinate pain management and fall prevention if anticoagulation increases bleeding risk with falls
- Lifestyle: diet, salt intake, exercise, alcohol, smoking — all impact BP and bone health
- Vaccinations as appropriate (flu, COVID, Tdap, others per age)

Suggested wording you can use with Dr. Wilson (quick script)
- “I’d like to review my blood pressure goals and whether my current meds are appropriate given my apixaban, osteopenia, and anemia. Can we check BMP, CBC, lipid panel, and an ECG today? If my BP is not at goal, what are the next steps and would you consider cardiology referral?”
- “My apixaban dose is X mg — is that the right dose for my current kidney function and weight? Can we check creatinine today and confirm?”

Next steps I can help with
- Draft a brief message or secure portal note to Dr. Wilson summarizing these questions and requesting labs/ECG or a cardiology referral if you want.
- Draft a visit checklist you can print or text to yourself.
- If you give me your exact creatinine/eGFR, weight, and the exact apixaban/metoprolol dosing and the indication for apixaban, I can give more specific guidance about apixaban dosing criteria and whether cardiology input is likely needed.

Would you like me to draft a short message to Dr. Wilson now (e.g., ask for labs, ECG, and your concerns about BP and possible cardiology referral)? If so, please confirm the medication doses and the creatinine value when you can.

